      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks James! I didn\u0027t realize spec PR doesn\u0027t include \"tracking of loads\". After talking with more folks I think this should be an expected behaviour. And yes, we don\u0027t need to do \"word-by-word\" tracking in such detailed level.\n\nTo align with you about my understanding, the validation rule is:\n\"all words accessible in any way through the struct definition to be initialized\". And an example for this is:\n- It doesn\u0027t need any statically access analysis for sub-members in immediate block (Assume struct A is declared as immediate var a).\n- If this a variable is used in entryPoint, then all slots except paddings (paddings between members, padding in members) are set.\n\nAnd yes, I\u0027ll follow below plan to do the inspector, great! \n\"we already know all of the transitively reference global variables in a given function (see sem::Function). The inspector should be able to just look for a referenced immediate variable in there and then do the recursion through the type hierarchy to get all of the non-padding bytes.\"",
      "parentUuid": "585a5a9d_5188ddc8",
      "revId": "7af3fa940e78fb6926e54385471e3f635ca7d5bb",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
